findings.suggested_fix:
Add value equality to Cell:
    def __eq__(self, other):
        return (isinstance(other, Cell) and self.ctype == other.ctype
                and self.value == other.value and self.xf_index == other.xf_index)
    def __ne__(self, other): return not self.__eq__(other)
    def __hash__(self): return hash((self.ctype, self.value, self.xf_index))
Safety verified: the existing tests relying on inequality (tests/test_sheet.py::test_cell, test_getitem_tuple; tests/test_workbook.py::test_getitem_ix/test_getitem_name) compare against PROFILEDEF (0,0)=text:'PROFIL' (XF:22) and (14,12)=number:267.0 (XF:21) — both non-empty, so assertNotEqual still holds and 84/84 keeps passing.

CAUTION — upstream xlrd 2.0.1 behaves identically, and the shipped tests only ever assert the negative form (tests/test_sheet.py:53-54,101-102 and tests/test_workbook.py:53-59 use assertNotEqual on non-empty cells 'PROFIL' / 267.0, so they stay green either way). If you want §27's pattern to work, add to Cell:
    def __eq__(self, other):
        return (isinstance(other, Cell) and self.ctype == other.ctype and self.value == other.value)
    __hash__ = None  # or a real __hash__ if hashability matters
Note xf_index must be excluded (empty_cell has xf_index None while formatting_info=True cells carry an int), and defining __eq__ on a __slots__ class makes instances unhashable unless __hash__ is supplied. Recommend leaving as-is for upstream parity unless a §27-shaped positive assertion is expected.
